PG.notes("s04-file-paths", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what a file path is, the difference between absolute paths (measured from the drive root) and relative paths (measured from the file you are editing), and the two special path tokens ./ and ../. The exercise has learners display five animal images stored in a deliberately tangled folder tree using only relative src paths.</p>\n<h3>What a file path is</h3>\n<ul><li>A file path is an address that points to exactly one file or folder in the computer's storage. It is written as a chain of folder names separated by slashes, ending at the target.</li><li>Comparable to giving street directions: country, then city, then district, then street, then building. Each step narrows the location until it is unique.</li><li>Everyday users rarely type paths because the file browser (Finder / Explorer) lets them click through folders. A path is the text version of that same clicking journey.</li><li>The destination of a path can be a file (cat.png, index.html) or a folder (Images/).</li></ul>\n<h3>The root of the file system</h3>\n<ul><li>The root is the top of the storage tree, i.e. the hard drive itself: C: on Windows, Macintosh HD on a Mac.</li><li>Every folder and file on the machine sits somewhere underneath the root, so the root is the natural starting point for an absolute path.</li></ul>\n<h3>Absolute file paths</h3>\n<ul><li>An absolute path starts at the root and lists every folder down to the target, e.g. Windows: C:\\Project\\Images\\cat.png, Mac: /Project/Images/cat.png.</li><li>Windows separates folders with backslashes and names the drive letter; macOS/Linux use forward slashes and a leading / for the root.</li><li>Because it always begins at the root, an absolute path works no matter which folder or file you are currently in.</li><li>Downside for websites: if the project folder is moved, renamed, or put on another machine/server, every absolute path breaks.</li></ul>\n<h3>Relative file paths (the one used in web development)</h3>\n<ul><li>A relative path is measured from the location of the file containing the code (e.g. the index.html you are writing in), not from the root.</li><li>Example tree: Project/ contains index.html, dog.png and Images/cat.png. From index.html the cat is simply Images/cat.png: go into Images, then pick cat.png.</li><li>Relative paths are usually shorter than absolute ones.</li><li>Key advantage: they survive moves. If the whole Project folder is nested inside another folder or copied to a server, the internal journey from index.html to Images/cat.png is unchanged, so the links still work.</li><li>Use relative paths whenever a page refers to your own resources: images, audio, video, or other HTML pages.</li></ul>\n<h3>Special characters: ../ (parent folder)</h3>\n<ul><li>Two dots followed by a slash at the start of a relative path mean: climb out of the current folder to the one above it, then continue from there.</li><li>Example: index.html lives in Project/, and essay.docx lives in the root above Project. From index.html the path is ../essay.docx: up one level into the root, then the file.</li><li>../ can be repeated to climb more than one level (../../file).</li><li>After climbing you can go back down into other folders: ../Folder1/fish.png means up one, then into Folder1, then fish.png.</li></ul>\n<h3>Special characters: ./ (current folder)</h3>\n<ul><li>A single dot followed by a slash means: start in the folder that directly contains the current file (the current directory).</li><li>Example: index.html and dog.png are both in Project/, so ./dog.png reaches the dog from index.html.</li><li>Anything at the same level of the tree as the current file can be reached with ./name.</li><li>The ./ can be left off (dog.png works the same in most cases) and you will see that style in other people's code. The course recommends always writing ./ because the shortened form does not resolve correctly in every environment.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The browser treats a path with no prefix as relative to the current document's folder, so ./dog.png and dog.png resolve to the same URL; tooling such as JavaScript module imports and some bundlers do require the explicit ./ though, which is why the habit is worth forming.</li></ul>\n<h3>Working out where a file sits in VS Code</h3>\n<ul><li>To find which folder contains a file, collapse folders in the Explorer sidebar. If collapsing a folder hides the file, that folder is its parent. If the file stays visible after collapsing everything, it lives in the top-level project folder.</li><li>Start typing a path inside src=&quot;&quot; and VS Code's autocomplete lists the files and folders reachable from that point. Selecting from the dropdown avoids typos (a single extra letter breaks the path).</li><li>Use the suggestions as a sanity check: if the dropdown does not show the folder you expect (e.g. typing ../ when you meant ./ lists dog.png, Folder0 and Folder1 instead of Folder3), the path prefix is wrong.</li><li>In the preview, a broken-image icon means the path does not point at a real file; rethink the path rather than the img tag.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reminder on the img element</h3>\n<ul><li>Images are added with the self-closing &lt;img&gt; element; src holds the path to the image and alt holds a text description.</li><li>Always add alt text so screen-reader users and anyone whose image fails to load still know what the picture shows.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Example tree used on the slides, with absolute and relative paths to the same files</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">Root (C:  or  Macintosh HD)\n├── essay.docx\n└── Project/\n    ├── index.html      &lt;- you are writing code here\n    ├── dog.png\n    └── Images/\n        └── cat.png\n\nAbsolute (Windows):  C:\\Project\\Images\\cat.png\nAbsolute (Mac):      /Project/Images/cat.png\nRelative from index.html:\n  Images/cat.png      (or ./Images/cat.png)\n  ./dog.png           same folder as index.html\n  ../essay.docx       up one level to the root</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The three path building blocks</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- ./  = the folder this HTML file is in --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;./dog.png&quot; alt=&quot;Dog&quot; /&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- folder/ = go down into a sub-folder --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;./Images/cat.png&quot; alt=&quot;Cat&quot; /&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ../ = go up one folder (stack them to go higher) --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;../essay.docx&quot;&gt;Essay&lt;/a&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Folder layout of the 4.0 File Paths exercise (reference repo folder '04.01 - File Paths')</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">4.0 File Paths/\n├── dog.png\n├── Folder0/\n│   ├── index.html      &lt;- write the code here\n│   ├── solution.html\n│   ├── goal.png\n│   ├── rabbit.png\n│   └── Folder3/\n│       └── cat.png\n└── Folder1/\n    ├── fish.png\n    └── Folder2/\n        └── bird.png</pre>\n<h2>The task: 4.0 File Paths: All the Animals</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Folder0/index.html contains an h1 'All the Animals' and five h2 headings (Rabbit:, Cat:, Dog:, Fish:, Bird:) with nothing under them. The five images are scattered across the folder tree shown above, and goal.png shows the finished page.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Under each h2, add an img whose src is a relative path from Folder0/index.html to the correct animal picture, so every image displays as in goal.png.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Extract the download, open it inside the Web Development Projects folder in VS Code, and expand every folder so the full tree is visible.</li><li>Rabbit (easiest, same folder as index.html): &lt;img src=&quot;./rabbit.png&quot; alt=&quot;Rabbit&quot; /&gt;.</li><li>Cat (one folder down): start in the current folder, go into Folder3, pick the file: ./Folder3/cat.png.</li><li>Dog (in the top-level exercise folder): collapse folders to confirm dog.png sits directly in 4.0 File Paths. From Folder0 climb one level with ../ and select the file: ../dog.png.</li><li>Fish: climb out of Folder0, then go into its sibling Folder1: ../Folder1/fish.png.</li><li>Bird: same route as the fish, one folder deeper: ../Folder1/Folder2/bird.png (copy the fish path and extend it).</li><li>Give every img an alt attribute naming the animal and close each with the self-closing /&gt;.</li><li>Use VS Code's path autocomplete while typing each src, and check the preview after each image; a broken-image icon means the path is wrong.</li><li>Compare with goal.png and then with solution.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The page shows the heading 'All the Animals' followed by Rabbit, Cat, Dog, Fish and Bird, each with its photo underneath and no broken-image icons.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;All the Animals&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n  &lt;h2&gt;Rabbit:&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;!-- same folder as index.html --&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;./rabbit.png&quot; alt=&quot;Rabbit&quot; /&gt;\n\n  &lt;h2&gt;Cat:&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;!-- down into Folder3 --&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;./Folder3/cat.png&quot; alt=&quot;Cat&quot; /&gt;\n\n  &lt;h2&gt;Dog:&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;!-- up out of Folder0 into the exercise root --&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;../dog.png&quot; alt=&quot;Dog&quot; /&gt;\n\n  &lt;h2&gt;Fish:&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;!-- up one, then down into Folder1 --&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;../Folder1/fish.png&quot; alt=&quot;Fish&quot; /&gt;\n\n  &lt;h2&gt;Bird:&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;!-- up one, down into Folder1, then Folder2 --&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;../Folder1/Folder2/bird.png&quot; alt=&quot;Bird&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing ./ with ../: ./ stays in the folder that holds the current file; ../ climbs one level above it. The lecture sometimes phrases ./ as moving 'up to the parent folder', meaning the folder that contains index.html, not the one above it.</li><li>Measuring the path from the project root or from where the image 'feels' it should be, rather than from the HTML file being edited.</li><li>Typos in folder or file names (one extra letter breaks the path). Prefer selecting from VS Code's autocomplete.</li><li>Using absolute paths like C:\\Users\\...\\cat.png in a web page: they only work on your own machine and break once the site is moved or deployed.</li><li>Leaving out alt text on the images.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s04-webpages", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how a multi-page website is just several HTML files in one project folder, joined by anchor tags whose href is a relative file path. Includes a four-option quiz on image paths and an exercise that links a home page to an About page (via a clickable image) and a Contact page (via a text link) stored in a public folder.</p>\n<h3>From one page to a multi-page site</h3>\n<ul><li>Up to now each project was one folder holding index.html (the starting file where all the code goes) plus supporting folders such as images.</li><li>A multi-page website adds more HTML files to the same project folder, e.g. about.html (information about the site owner) and contact.html (how to reach them).</li><li>Extra pages are only reachable if some page links to them, so each page needs anchor (&lt;a&gt;) elements pointing at the others.</li><li>index.html acts as the home page of the site.</li></ul>\n<h3>Linking to another page with a relative path</h3>\n<ul><li>Use the same &lt;a&gt; element as for external links, but put a relative file path in href instead of a full URL.</li><li>If about.html sits next to index.html, the link written inside index.html is &lt;a href=&quot;./about.html&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;: ./ means look in the folder containing index.html, then open about.html.</li><li>Clicking the rendered link makes the browser load about.html in place of the home page; the browser Back button returns to index.html.</li><li>The path is always measured from the file that contains the link, exactly as in the file paths lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Quiz recap: displaying assets/images/cat.png from index.html</h3>\n<ul><li>Setup: index.html is in the Project folder, which also contains assets/, which contains images/, which contains cat.png.</li><li>Option with src=&quot;../assets/images/cat.png&quot; is wrong: ../ climbs out of the Project folder into its parent, where there is no assets folder.</li><li>Option with &lt;img href=...&gt; is wrong: images use the src attribute; href belongs to anchor tags.</li><li>Option with &lt;a href=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot;&gt; is wrong: an anchor creates a clickable link, it does not display the picture on the page.</li><li>Correct: &lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot; /&gt;, i.e. start in the Project folder, go into assets, then images, then the file.</li><li>If the quiz was difficult, revise file paths before continuing; later lessons depend on them.</li></ul>\n<h3>Typical project layout: assets and public</h3>\n<ul><li>assets/ holds non-HTML resources; inside it an images/ folder holds pictures (assets/images/cat.png).</li><li>public/ conventionally holds every HTML page other than the home page (about.html, contact.html). index.html stays at the top level of the project.</li><li>VS Code's Explorer may compact a folder that only contains another folder into one row (shown as assets/images); it is still two nested folders.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The single-row display is VS Code's 'Compact Folders' setting (explorer.compactFolders); turning it off shows each folder on its own line.</li></ul>\n<h3>Previewing the goal without seeing the answer</h3>\n<ul><li>Right-click solution.html in the Explorer and choose Show Preview to see the finished page working, including its links, without opening the solution source.</li><li>Left-clicking solution.html would open its code and reveal the answer, so avoid that until you have attempted the task.</li><li>Run Show Preview on index.html while working to test each link as you add it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Any element can be a link: wrapping an image in &lt;a&gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>An anchor's content does not have to be text. Put an &lt;img&gt; element between &lt;a&gt; and &lt;/a&gt; and the whole picture becomes clickable.</li><li>The href on the &lt;a&gt; decides where the click goes; the src on the &lt;img&gt; decides which picture is shown. They are two separate paths, each measured from index.html.</li><li>Suggested personal touch: drop a photo of yourself into assets/images and point the img src at it instead of the cat.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Multi-page project structure used in the exercise (reference repo folder '04.02 - Webpages')</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">4.1 Webpages/\n├── index.html          &lt;- home page, write code here\n├── solution.html\n├── goal.png\n├── assets/\n│   └── images/\n│       └── cat.png\n└── public/\n    ├── about.html\n    └── contact.html</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The four quiz options (only the last one is correct)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- 1. wrong: ../ leaves the Project folder --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;../assets/images/cat.png&quot; /&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- 2. wrong: img has no href, it needs src --&gt;\n&lt;img href=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot; /&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- 3. wrong: an anchor links to the file, it doesn't show it --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot;&gt;cat&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- 4. correct --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Text link vs image link</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- text link to a page in the same folder --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;./about.html&quot;&gt;About Me&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- image link: the &lt;img&gt; is the clickable content of the &lt;a&gt; --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;./about.html&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot; alt=&quot;Cat&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/a&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 4.1 Webpages: link the home page to About and Contact</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html already contains an h1 'Welcome to My Website!', an hr, and two HTML comments marking where to add the image link (above the hr) and the contact link (below it). public/about.html (heading plus placeholder paragraphs) and public/contact.html (heading, phone, email, address) are already written.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the home page navigate to both sub-pages: a picture that opens about.html when clicked, and a 'Contact Me' text link that opens contact.html.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Extract the zip, open it in VS Code inside the Web Development Projects folder, and expand all folders to see assets/images/cat.png and public/about.html, public/contact.html.</li><li>Right-click solution.html, choose Show Preview, and click around to see the target behaviour.</li><li>Open Show Preview on index.html so changes can be tested live.</li><li>Start with the simpler task. Under the hr add an anchor whose href goes from index.html into public and opens contact.html: &lt;a href=&quot;./public/contact.html&quot;&gt;Contact Me&lt;/a&gt;. Click it in the preview to check it opens the contact page, then go Back.</li><li>Above the hr, create an anchor pointing to the about page: &lt;a href=&quot;./public/about.html&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.</li><li>Instead of text, place an img inside that anchor with src=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot; (or a photo of yourself copied into assets/images) and an alt description.</li><li>Click the image in the preview; it should load about.html.</li><li>Optionally replace the cat with your own photo.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Home page shows the heading, a clickable picture that leads to the About Me page, a horizontal rule, and a 'Contact Me' link that leads to the contact page.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Website!&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Add an image of yourself that links to the about page --&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;./public/about.html&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/cat.png&quot; alt=&quot;Cat&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;/a&gt;\n\n  &lt;hr /&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Add a link to your contact me page here --&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;./public/contact.html&quot;&gt;Contact Me&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing the image path with the dot and slash swapped (/.assets/...) or with a leading / instead of ./; the correct form is ./assets/images/cat.png.</li><li>Using href on an img or src on an a: images need src, links need href.</li><li>Expecting an &lt;a&gt; pointing at an image file to display the image; it only creates a link to it.</li><li>Forgetting that pages inside public/ need the public/ segment in the path when linked from index.html.</li><li>Left-clicking solution.html (opens the answer code) when the aim was to preview it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l027", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks line by line through the standard HTML5 boilerplate (doctype, html with lang, head with charset meta and title, body), explains nesting and indentation, and shows the VS Code ! shortcut that generates the boilerplate. Ends with a free-form 'build your own burger' exercise using invented, properly nested elements.</p>\n<h3>What a boilerplate is</h3>\n<ul><li>Every HTML file follows the same outer skeleton, the way a formal letter always has an address block, greeting, body and sign-off. That reusable starting skeleton is called the boilerplate.</li><li>Start every new HTML file from it, then add page content inside the body.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;: the doctype declaration</h3>\n<ul><li>Must be the very first line of the file. Its job is to declare the HTML version used, so the browser parses the document correctly.</li><li>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt; (angle bracket, exclamation mark, DOCTYPE in capitals, then html) declares HTML5, the current version.</li><li>It is a declaration, not an element: it has no closing tag.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;: the root element</h3>\n<ul><li>The html element is the root of the document. Every other element (headings, images, links, head, body) goes between &lt;html&gt; and &lt;/html&gt;.</li><li>The lang attribute states the language of the text inside the element, e.g. lang=&quot;en&quot; for English.</li><li>Sighted visitors don't notice it; screen readers and other assistive technology read it to choose the right pronunciation when reading the page aloud.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> lang also helps search engines and browser translation features, and can be set on individual elements for passages in another language.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;head&gt;: information about the page, not shown on it</h3>\n<ul><li>The head holds information the browser needs to render the page correctly. Nothing in it (no text, images or links) appears in the page content area.</li><li>Two items always belong in the head: the character-set meta tag and the title.</li><li>&lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt; sets the character encoding so every character displays correctly. Some encodings can't show emoji or symbols such as × and ÷; UTF-8 is one of the most common and covers them, so the line is normally copied in unchanged.</li><li>&lt;title&gt;My Website&lt;/title&gt;: the text between the title tags is what the browser shows on the tab.</li><li>Many more tags can go in the head (the Google home page has lots of them); others will be introduced as they come up.</li><li>To inspect a real site's boilerplate in Chrome, right-click the page and choose View Page Source: doctype, html, head, charset and title are all visible near the top.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;body&gt;: all visible content</h3>\n<ul><li>The body comes after the head (like a person: head, then body).</li><li>Everything the user sees goes between &lt;body&gt; and &lt;/body&gt;: headings, paragraphs, images, links and every other content or structure element.</li><li>This is where almost all the work in the HTML sections of the course happens.</li></ul>\n<h3>Nesting and indentation</h3>\n<ul><li>Elements sit inside other elements: head and body inside html, h1 and img inside body. This is nesting, and it becomes increasingly important throughout the course.</li><li>The analogy used is a burger: html is the top and bottom bun, body is a pair of tomato slices inside it, and the page content is sandwiched between those.</li><li>A short element whose opening and closing tags fit easily on one line (e.g. &lt;title&gt;My Website&lt;/title&gt;) can stay on one line; it still has a start and an end.</li><li>Indent each nested level (two spaces in the slides) so every closing tag lines up vertically with its opening tag. Correct indentation shows at a glance which element contains which and keeps code tidy.</li><li>When starting out, type the boilerplate by hand a few times to learn it before relying on the shortcut.</li></ul>\n<h3>VS Code shortcut: ! then Enter</h3>\n<ul><li>In a file saved with the .html extension, type ! and press Enter on the first suggestion; VS Code (via its built-in Emmet) inserts the whole boilerplate.</li><li>It only works once the file has been named/saved as something.html; with any other extension the suggestion does not appear.</li><li>The generated version contains two extra meta tags not covered above.</li><li>&lt;meta http-equiv=&quot;X-UA-Compatible&quot; content=&quot;IE=edge&quot;&gt; exists only for Internet Explorer compatibility. IE is retired, so this line can be deleted to keep the file clean.</li><li>&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt; controls how the page is sized relative to the screen it opens on, i.e. how the browser displays it initially.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> width=device-width makes the layout width match the device's screen width instead of a phone pretending to be ~980px wide, and initial-scale=1.0 means no zoom on load. This matters a lot once responsive CSS is introduced. Recent Emmet versions already leave out the X-UA-Compatible line.</li><li>The default &lt;title&gt;Document&lt;/title&gt; should be replaced with a real page title.</li></ul>\n<h3>Attributes refresher</h3>\n<ul><li>An attribute goes inside the opening tag as name=&quot;value&quot; (name, equals sign, value in quotes).</li><li>An element can carry as many attributes as needed, separated by spaces.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Minimal HTML5 boilerplate, annotated</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;               &lt;!-- HTML5 doctype: first line, no closing tag --&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;              &lt;!-- root element; lang helps screen readers --&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;                      &lt;!-- page info, not displayed --&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt;  &lt;!-- character encoding --&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My Website&lt;/title&gt; &lt;!-- text on the browser tab --&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;                      &lt;!-- all visible content goes here --&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">What VS Code's ! + Enter generates (Emmet); the X-UA-Compatible line can be deleted</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;meta http-equiv=&quot;X-UA-Compatible&quot; content=&quot;IE=edge&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Document&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    \n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Build-your-own-burger exercise: invented elements practising nesting, attributes and indentation (illustrative)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;bun type=&quot;brioche&quot; toasted=&quot;yes&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;sauce flavour=&quot;chipotle&quot;&gt;&lt;/sauce&gt;\n  &lt;lettuce leaves=&quot;2&quot;&gt;&lt;/lettuce&gt;\n  &lt;patty kind=&quot;bean&quot; cooked=&quot;well-done&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;cheese style=&quot;smoked&quot;&gt;&lt;/cheese&gt;\n  &lt;/patty&gt;\n  &lt;tomato slices=&quot;3&quot;&gt;&lt;/tomato&gt;\n  &lt;pickles&gt;&lt;/pickles&gt;\n&lt;/bun&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Build your own burger (nesting practice)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> No starter files. Open VS Code and create any new .html file (or practise in the same file where you typed out the boilerplate).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Describe an imaginary burger as HTML-style code using invented elements, to practise opening/closing tags, attributes and nesting with clean indentation.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>First, type the full boilerplate from memory in a new .html file, then try the ! + Enter shortcut and compare the two.</li><li>Create an outer bun element with an opening and closing tag, and give it one or more attributes in name=&quot;value&quot; form.</li><li>Inside the bun, add whatever fillings you like as their own elements (tomato, lettuce, patty, cheese, sauce...), each with opening and closing tags.</li><li>Nest at least one element inside another filling to create a deeper level.</li><li>Indent each nested level so every closing tag lines up with its opening tag.</li><li>Optionally share your burger code in the course Q&amp;A.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A neatly indented block where it is obvious which elements sit inside which, and every opening tag has a matching closing tag. (Invented tags won't render as anything meaningful; the point is the structure.)</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting visible content (headings, images, paragraphs) inside head instead of body.</li><li>Typing ! in a file that hasn't been saved with a .html extension and wondering why no boilerplate appears.</li><li>Leaving the auto-generated title as 'Document'.</li><li>Poor indentation that hides which element a closing tag belongs to; mismatched or missing closing tags in nested code.</li><li>Treating &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt; as an element and trying to close it, or placing anything above it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s04-portfolio", "<p class=\"summary\">End-of-section project: build an HTML-only web developer portfolio that links to the Movie Ranking (section 2) and Birthday Invite (section 3) projects, shows a screenshot preview of each, and links to About Me and Contact Me pages. Brings together the boilerplate, headings, hr, anchors, images and relative paths from the section.</p>\n<h3>Why a portfolio</h3>\n<ul><li>Developers and designers keep a personal site that shows what they have built and links to each piece of work, so potential employers or clients can browse it and get in touch.</li><li>This version is deliberately simple (HTML only). It gets restyled once CSS is covered and is put online in the next lesson, so treat it as a real, public page: use your own content.</li></ul>\n<h3>Project folder layout</h3>\n<ul><li>index.html at the top level is the portfolio home page; it starts with TODO comments describing each step.</li><li>public/ holds the other pages: movie-ranking.html, birthday-invite.html, about.html, contact.html. The supplied project pages are the standard versions; overwrite them with your own code from the earlier sections, since yours will have your dates, films and customisations.</li><li>assets/images/ holds the preview screenshots. Ready-made screenshots of both projects are included as a fallback.</li><li>goal.png shows the target: title, subtitle, hr, two project links each with a preview image, another hr, then About Me and Contact Me links.</li></ul>\n<h3>Making preview images with screenshots</h3>\n<ul><li>The skill needed is taking a screenshot on your OS (search for the method for Windows, Mac or Linux if unsure).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Common shortcuts: Mac Cmd+Shift+4 (drag a region; Cmd+Shift+5 for options), Windows Win+Shift+S (Snipping Tool), most Linux desktops PrtSc or Shift+PrtSc.</li><li>Workflow: right-click a project file (e.g. public/birthday-invite.html) &gt; Show Preview, screenshot the rendered preview, drag the image file into assets/images, and rename it to something sensible.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Prefer lowercase, hyphenated file names with no spaces or apostrophes (birthday-invite.png). Names with spaces work locally but must be URL-encoded (%20) on a web server and are easy to mistype.</li></ul>\n<h3>Controlling image size with the height attribute</h3>\n<ul><li>A raw screenshot is usually large and can fill the whole page.</li><li>Add height=&quot;200&quot; to the img element: the image renders 200 pixels tall and the browser works out the width automatically, keeping the original proportions.</li><li>The starter file links to the MDN documentation for the img element's height attribute for further reading.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Set only one of width/height to keep the aspect ratio; setting both to values that don't match the original ratio distorts the image.</li></ul>\n<h3>Linking the project pages</h3>\n<ul><li>Each project title is an anchor whose href is the relative path from index.html into public/, e.g. ./public/movie-ranking.html and ./public/birthday-invite.html.</li><li>In the instructor's version the anchor is placed inside an h3 so the link text displays at heading size: &lt;h3&gt;&lt;a href=...&gt;Movie Ranking Project&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;.</li><li>The preview img goes below its heading, with src pointing into ./assets/images/.</li><li>About Me and Contact Me links at the bottom point to ./public/about.html and ./public/contact.html; edit those two pages so they describe you.</li></ul>\n<h3>Breaking a big job into TODOs</h3>\n<ul><li>Professional developers split a large task into a list of small ones. The starter index.html does this with numbered TODO comments that can be ticked off one at a time.</li><li>Beyond the required parts, any HTML elements learned so far can be added; this is your page.</li></ul>\n<h3>Instructor's solution notes</h3>\n<ul><li>Starts from the boilerplate with the unnecessary meta tags removed (the IE compatibility line) and a custom title.</li><li>Uses an h1 for the name, an h2 subtitle, and hr elements to separate the sections.</li><li>Each project: an h3 containing the link, followed by its preview image; About and Contact links at the very bottom.</li><li>It is only one example; layout and wording are up to you.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Project structure (reference repo folder '04.03 - HTML Portfolio Project')</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">4.3 HTML Portfolio Project/\n├── index.html          &lt;- portfolio home page (TODOs)\n├── solution.html\n├── goal.png\n├── assets/\n│   └── images/         &lt;- project screenshots go here\n└── public/\n    ├── movie-ranking.html\n    ├── birthday-invite.html\n    ├── about.html\n    └── contact.html</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Height-only sizing keeps proportions</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- 200px tall, width calculated automatically --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/movie-ranking.png&quot; alt=&quot;Movie ranking page preview&quot; height=&quot;200&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 4.3 HTML Portfolio Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Downloaded project zip: index.html containing only TODO comments, public/ with stock versions of movie-ranking.html, birthday-invite.html, about.html and contact.html, assets/images/ with two stock screenshots, plus goal.png and solution.html.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> An HTML-only portfolio home page with a title and subtitle, a linked heading and preview image for each of the two earlier projects, and links to About Me and Contact Me, all using relative paths.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Learn how to take a screenshot on your computer if you don't already know.</li><li>Extract the zip, open it in VS Code, and look at goal.png.</li><li>TODO: create the HTML boilerplate in index.html (type it or use ! + Enter), removing the unneeded IE meta tag.</li><li>TODO: set the title element to your own page title.</li><li>TODO: paste your own Movie Ranking and Birthday Invite code into public/movie-ranking.html and public/birthday-invite.html, replacing the stock code.</li><li>TODO: preview each project (right-click &gt; Show Preview), screenshot it, move the screenshot into assets/images and give it a clear name (or use the provided images).</li><li>TODO: add an h1 with your name/portfolio title and an h2 subtitle such as 'I'm a Web Developer', followed by an hr.</li><li>TODO: for each project add a heading containing a link to its page: &lt;h3&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;./public/movie-ranking.html&quot;&gt;Movie Ranking Project&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;, and the same for ./public/birthday-invite.html.</li><li>TODO: under each heading add the preview img with src=&quot;./assets/images/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, an alt description and height=&quot;200&quot; so it doesn't fill the page.</li><li>Finally add another hr and links to ./public/about.html and ./public/contact.html, then personalise those two pages.</li><li>Click every link in the preview to confirm each page opens.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Matches goal.png: large name heading, 'I'm a Web Developer' subtitle, hr, blue underlined 'Movie Ranking Project' link with a 200px-tall screenshot below, the same for 'Birthday Invite Project', hr, then 'About Me' and 'Contact Me' links side by side.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;HTML Portfolio Project&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Your Name's Portfolio&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;I'm a Web Developer&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;hr /&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- project 1: linked heading + preview --&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;./public/movie-ranking.html&quot;&gt;Movie Ranking Project&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/movie-ranking.png&quot;\n         alt=&quot;Preview of the movie ranking page&quot; height=&quot;200&quot; /&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- project 2 --&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;./public/birthday-invite.html&quot;&gt;Birthday Invite Project&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/birthday-invite.png&quot;\n         alt=&quot;Preview of the birthday invite page&quot; height=&quot;200&quot; /&gt;\n\n    &lt;hr /&gt;\n    &lt;a href=&quot;./public/about.html&quot;&gt;About Me&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;a href=&quot;./public/contact.html&quot;&gt;Contact Me&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the public/ segment in the project links (./movie-ranking.html instead of ./public/movie-ranking.html).</li><li>Full-size screenshots swamping the page; add height=&quot;200&quot; (and only height, so proportions are kept).</li><li>Screenshot file names with spaces or apostrophes that are hard to type and break when mistyped; rename them.</li><li>Leaving the stock project code, placeholder About text and fake contact details in place when the site is about to be published.</li><li>Inside public/ pages, any link back to the home page needs ../index.html, not ./index.html.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l029", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains web hosting (moving a site's files from your own machine onto an always-online web server) and then walks through publishing the HTML Portfolio project for free with GitHub Pages: create a public repository, upload the folder's contents, and switch Pages on for the main branch.</p>\n<h3>Local development vs web hosting</h3>\n<ul><li>Local development: every HTML file, image and folder sits on your own computer, so only you can open the site.</li><li>Sharing a local site would mean handing over a copy of the files (like the course's zipped downloads), which is not a real website.</li><li>A web server is a computer permanently connected to the internet that serves the files it holds to anyone who requests them.</li><li>Web hosting = putting your site's files (index.html, other pages, images) onto such a server so the site gets a public address.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why index.html matters</h3>\n<ul><li>GitHub Pages serves the file named index.html at the top level of the repository as the home page.</li><li>The name must be exactly index.html, all lowercase. home.html, me.html or Index.html will not become the landing page.</li><li>Upload your finished index.html, not the starter version that only had TODO comments.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Most web servers follow the same convention: a folder URL returns that folder's index.html.</li></ul>\n<h3>GitHub repository settings used</h3>\n<ul><li>Repository name: html-portfolio (it becomes part of the live URL). Description is optional.</li><li>Visibility must be Public so anyone can view the hosted site.</li><li>Tick 'Add a README file' at creation. The repo then opens on the normal file view with Add file &gt; Upload files available.</li><li>Clicking Commit changes after uploading records the files on the main branch at the root (/), which is what Pages publishes. Git itself is covered properly in a later section.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The live address follows the pattern https://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/html-portfolio/. Free Pages hosting needs a public repo.</li></ul>\n<h3>Relative paths make hosting just work</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The portfolio's relative links (./public/..., ./assets/images/...) work identically locally and on github.io as long as the folder structure is uploaded unchanged; an absolute path to your own disk would break once hosted.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The Pages server is case-sensitive: Images/Preview.png will not find images/preview.png, even if it worked on your Mac or PC.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Folder contents to upload (drag these items, not the parent folder)</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">index.html          &lt;- your finished portfolio home page (exact name, lowercase)\npublic/\n  about.html\n  contact.html\n  movie-ranking.html\n  birthday-invite.html\nassets/\n  images/\n    movie-ranking.png\n    birthday-invite.png</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> How the live URL maps onto the uploaded files</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">https://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/html-portfolio/                     -&gt; index.html\nhttps://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/html-portfolio/public/about.html    -&gt; public/about.html\nhttps://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/html-portfolio/assets/images/x.png  -&gt; assets/images/x.png</pre>\n<h2>The task: Put the HTML Portfolio online with GitHub Pages</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The completed HTML Portfolio project from the previous lesson on your computer: index.html with your code, a public folder with the other pages, and assets/images with the preview screenshots.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A live, shareable URL that shows the portfolio exactly as it looks locally, with every link and image working.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Go to github.com and sign up for a free account, or sign in if you already have one.</li><li>Use the + menu in the top-right corner and choose New repository.</li><li>Name it html-portfolio, optionally add a description, set visibility to Public, and tick Add a README file. Click Create repository.</li><li>On the new repo page, open the Add file dropdown and choose Upload files.</li><li>In your file manager, open the portfolio project folder. Confirm index.html is spelled exactly that, lowercase, and contains your finished code; confirm the images folder holds the previews and public holds the other pages.</li><li>Select the contents of the project folder (index.html, public, assets) and drag them into the upload area. Do not drag the outer folder itself.</li><li>Click Commit changes. Processing can take a few minutes; refresh until every file and folder appears in the repo listing. Re-upload anything missing.</li><li>Open the repo's Settings tab, then Pages in the sidebar.</li><li>Under Build and deployment &gt; Branch, change None to main (root folder /) and click Save.</li><li>Refresh the Pages screen until a banner says the site is live at a github.io address. This can take roughly 1 to 10 minutes; if you see an error such as no server being available, wait and try again.</li><li>Click Visit site, click through every page and image to check it matches the local version, then share the URL (the course suggests posting it in the Q&amp;A).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A public github.io URL serving the portfolio home page, with working links to the project pages, About Me and Contact Me, and visible preview images.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Dragging the whole project folder into the upload box. The repo root then has no index.html, so the Pages URL shows a 404 or the README instead of the portfolio.</li><li>Home page renamed (home.html, me.html) or capitalised (Index.html).</li><li>Uploading the untouched starter index.html.</li><li>Skipping the README checkbox, which leaves the empty-repo setup screen instead of the upload option.</li><li>Expecting the site instantly. Deployment can take several minutes, and transient server errors usually clear on retry.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l030", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what the course's capstone projects are and how they differ from the guided projects: you get a client-style spec and an example site, but no starter files, walkthrough or solution, and the result should be original enough to go in a portfolio.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Employers judge developers mainly on what they have built, so a portfolio of real projects matters more than a list of courses.</li><li>GitHub is where the portfolio starts: you can pin chosen repositories on your profile, and the contribution graph shows how regularly you push code.</li><li>Each capstone is scoped to the skills taught so far, but expect to get stuck and to look things up in docs or online. That struggle is intended.</li><li>Capstones work like a client brief: a written specification plus an example site showing the target. No starter code, no solution files, no walkthrough video.</li><li>Do not clone the example site. Projects that are obviously copied from a popular tutorial are easy for reviewers to spot; build your own version with your own content and design choices.</li><li>The aim is to break out of relying on tutorials and to see your own progress from the first capstone to the later ones.</li><li>Capstones are optional if you are not aiming for a developer job. Share finished work in the Q&amp;A.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Copying the example capstone site or tutorial code into a portfolio.</li><li>Skipping the struggle by searching for a finished solution instead of working from the spec and documentation.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l031", "<p class=\"summary\">Written brief for Capstone Project 1: build a plain-HTML online resume as a multi-page site, then publish it on GitHub Pages. There is no solution code or walkthrough; the work is meant to be the learner's own.</p>\n<h3>Required page structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Single entry file named index.html, starting with the &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt; declaration.</li><li>A head containing a title element whose text is 'My Resume'.</li><li>The body holds the resume sections: summary or objective, education (degrees, schools, dates), work experience (titles, employers, dates, responsibilities), skills, and awards or certifications.</li><li>Section headings use heading elements; collections of items use ul or ol lists.</li><li>Include a profile photo with an image element.</li><li>Anchor tags link to additional pages (for example Hobbies and Contact), which makes the resume a multi-page site.</li><li>Finish with a footer element carrying the owner's name and any copyright or disclaimer text. The brief points to the MDN footer reference for details.</li></ul>\n<h3>Constraints and expectations</h3>\n<ul><li>HTML only: no CSS or JavaScript, even if the learner already knows them. This keeps the project within scope.</li><li>Treat it like professional work: look up unfamiliar elements in MDN or search rather than expecting every answer in the course.</li><li>Open index.html in a browser to confirm it renders correctly.</li><li>Push the project to GitHub, publish via GitHub Pages, share the link in the course Q&amp;A, and leave feedback on other students' sites.</li></ul>\n<h3>FAQ takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Capstones can go in a portfolio or be shown in interviews, because they have no official solution, so the result is original work. Course projects with published solutions are easier to recognise as course material.</li><li>Getting stuck is the point: gaps found while building show which earlier lessons or docs to revisit, since watching videos alone rarely cements a skill.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Minimal skeleton matching the brief (own illustrative code)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My Resume&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Your Name&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;images/me.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Profile photo&quot; width=&quot;200&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Summary&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;...&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Education&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Work Experience&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Skills&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Awards&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;a href=&quot;hobbies.html&quot;&gt;Hobbies&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=&quot;contact.html&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n    &lt;footer&gt;&amp;copy; Your Name&lt;/footer&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Capstone 1: Online Resume</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty project; the learner creates index.html from scratch (no starter files, no goal image beyond a linked example site).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A multi-page, HTML-only online resume published on GitHub Pages.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create index.html with the doctype and a head whose title is 'My Resume'.</li><li>Write the body sections: summary, education, work experience, skills, awards/certifications.</li><li>Use headings for section titles and ul/ol for lists.</li><li>Add a profile photo with an img element.</li><li>Create extra pages (e.g. hobbies.html, contact.html) and link them with anchor tags.</li><li>Add a footer with name and copyright text.</li><li>Open in a browser to check it.</li><li>Push to GitHub and enable GitHub Pages.</li><li>Post the link in the Q&amp;A and comment on others' projects.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A working HTML-only resume site, reachable via a GitHub Pages URL, with links between pages.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using CSS or JS, which the brief excludes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Broken relative links or image paths when pages sit in different folders; revisit the file paths lesson.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> GitHub Pages expects the entry file to be named index.html in lowercase.</li></ul>");
